'''Philip Brian Scott''' (born August 4, 1958) is an American politician, stock car racer, and businessman who has served since 2017 as the 82nd [[governor of Vermont]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he was a representative for the [[Washington Vermont Senate District|Washington District]] in the [[Vermont Senate]] from 2001 to 2011 and the 81st [[Lieutenant Governor of Vermont|lieutenant governor of Vermont]] from 2011 to 2017.

Scott was first elected governor of Vermont in the [[2016 Vermont gubernatorial election|2016 general election]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vermont Election Results |url=https://vtelectionresults.sec.state.vt.us/Index.html#/state |website=Vermont Secretary of State |access-date=November 8, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170811143454/https://vtelectionresults.sec.state.vt.us/Index.html#/state |archive-date=August 11, 2017 |url-status=dead |df=mdy-all }}</ref> and was reelected in [[2018 Vermont gubernatorial election|2018]], [[2020 Vermont gubernatorial election|2020]], [[2022 Vermont gubernatorial election|2022]], and [[2024 Vermont gubernatorial election|2024]]. Scott's 2024 margin of victory is the largest in any Vermont gubernatorial election since [[1946 Vermont gubernatorial election|1946]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Heintz |first1=Paul |title=Scott's Victory Lap: Gov Wins Third Term, Gray Elected LG, Speaker Johnson Falls Short |url=https://www.sevendaysvt.com/OffMessage/archives/2020/11/04/scotts-victory-lap-gov-wins-third-term-gray-elected-lg-speaker-johnson-falls-short?cb=1604482319 |website=Seven Days |access-date=4 November 2020}}</ref> As of 2025, Scott is the [[List of current United States governors|second-longest serving]] incumbent governor in the U.S.

Scott is often described as a [[Moderate Republicans (modern United States)|moderate Republican]] or a [[Rockefeller Republican|liberal Republican]]. While according to the [[Cook Partisan Voting Index]], Vermont is the most [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]]-leaning U.S. state (D+17), Scott is one of the nation's most popular governors.<ref>{{Cite web|last=eyokley|date=2021-11-18|title=Why the Country's Most Popular Governor May Be Resisting the GOP's Calls to Run for Senate in Vermont|url=https://morningconsult.com/2021/11/18/phil-scott-approval-vermont-polling-senate-race/|access-date=2021-11-18|website=Morning Consult|language=en-US}}</ref>

== Early life ==
Philip B. Scott was born on August 4, 1958, in [[Barre (city), Vermont|Barre, Vermont]], the son of Marian (Beckley) and Howard Roy Scott (1914–1969).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://elections.wcax.com/vt-races/governor/ |title=Vermont Statewide Races: Governor |date=2016 |website=Election 2016 |publisher=WCAX-TV |location=South Burlington, VT |access-date=December 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161222224255/http://elections.wcax.com/vt-races/governor/ |archive-date=December 22, 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.uvm.edu/newsstories/news/bridge_builders|title = Search &#124; the University of Vermont}}</ref> His father was disabled after being wounded while serving in [[World War II]] and later worked as a vehicle permit supervisor for the state highway department.<ref>{{cite news |date=December 3, 1969 |title=Obituary, Howard R. Scott |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/120017739/obituary-for-howard-r-scott/ |work=[[Barre Montpelier Times Argus]] |location=Barre, VT |page=2 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Lyman |first=Dorothy |date=December 22, 1969 |title=What Others Say: A Tribute to Howard R. Scott |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/120017928/scotty/ |work=[[Barre Montpelier Times Argus]] |location=Barre, VT |page=4 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> In 1973, Scott's mother married Robert F. Dubois (1919–1983).<ref>{{cite news |date=April 28, 1983 |title=Obituary, Robert F. DuBois |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/120017492/obituary-for-robert-f-dubois/ |work=[[The Burlington Free Press]] |location=Burlington, VT |page=2B |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/1606/images/31394_207270-01256?treeid=&personid=&hintid=&queryId=987806de38c270c4bc6f5416e50c3295&usePUB=true&_phsrc=CAH24&_phstart=successSource&usePUBJs=true&_gl=1*1cwqqyt*_ga*MTk0MjIxNjk5Ny4xNjc3MTUzOTAy*_ga_4QT8FMEX30*MTY3Nzc2NjY3OC40LjEuMTY3Nzc2NzExMC42MC4wLjA.&pId=417959 |title=Vermont Marriage Records, 1909-2008, Entry for Robert Farnam Dubois and Marion Scott |date=1974 |website=Ancestry.com |publisher=Ancestry.com LLC |location=Lehi, UT |access-date=March 2, 2023 |url-access=subscription}}</ref>

Scott graduated from Barre's [[Spaulding High School (Barre, Vermont)|Spaulding High School]] in 1976.<ref>{{cite news |last=Savage |first=Katy |date=October 1, 2015 |title=Milne Cedes Gov. Run To Friend Scott |url=http://www.thevermontstandard.com/2015/10/milne-cedes-gov-run-to-friend-scott/ |newspaper=Vermont Standard |location=Woodstock, VT |access-date=December 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161222222623/http://www.thevermontstandard.com/2015/10/milne-cedes-gov-run-to-friend-scott/ |archive-date=December 22, 2016 |url-status=dead |df=mdy-all }}</ref> Scott is a 1980 graduate of the [[University of Vermont]], where he received a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in [[industrial education]].<ref>{{cite news |date=September 17, 2015 |title=Phil Scott Says Yes at Labor Day Parade in Northfield |url=http://www.thenorthfieldnews.com/news/2015-09-17/Front_Page/Phil_Scott_Says_Yes_at_Labor_Day_Parade_in_Northfi.html |newspaper=Northfield News |location=Northfield, VT |access-date=December 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161222223143/http://www.thenorthfieldnews.com/news/2015-09-17/Front_Page/Phil_Scott_Says_Yes_at_Labor_Day_Parade_in_Northfi.html |archive-date=December 22, 2016 |url-status=usurped }}</ref>

== Business career ==
After graduating from high school, Scott began working at DuBois Construction, a [[Middlesex, Vermont|Middlesex]] business founded by the brother of his mother's second husband.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hewitt |first=Elizabeth |date=January 13, 2017 |title=Governor Details Construction Company Sale |url=https://vtdigger.org/2017/01/13/governor-details-construction-company-sale/ |newspaper=VT Digger |location=Montpelier, VT |ref={{sfnRef|Governor Details Construction Company Sale}} |access-date=January 15, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170116173829/https://vtdigger.org/2017/01/13/governor-details-construction-company-sale/ |archive-date=January 16, 2017 |url-status=live |df=mdy-all }}</ref> After college, Scott owned a motorcycle sales and repair shop in [[Morrisville, Vermont|Morrisville]], then worked as a construction manager for Morrisville's H. A. Manosh Corporation.<ref>{{cite news |last=McCullum |first=April |date=February 28, 2017 |title=Scott seeks reform of Act 250, which he says foiled shop plan |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-burlington-free-press-shop-plan/127532601/ |work=[[The Burlington Free Press]] |location=Burlington, VT |page=A4 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> Scott has also been involved in other business ventures, including ownership of a restaurant and a nightclub.<ref>{{cite news |date=December 23, 1996 |title=Career Spotlight: Phil Scott |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-burlington-free-press-legends-nightc/119956100/ |work=[[The Burlington Free Press]]: Business Monday |location=Burlington, VT |page=6 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref>

Scott became a co-owner of DuBois Construction in 1986.<ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Mark |date=September 25, 2016 |title=Updated: Scott to sell share in construction firm if elected governor |url=https://vtdigger.org/2016/09/25/breaking-news-scott-sell-share-construction-firm-elected-governor/ |newspaper=VT Digger |location=Montpelier, VT |access-date=December 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161223063743/https://vtdigger.org/2016/09/25/breaking-news-scott-sell-share-construction-firm-elected-governor/ |archive-date=December 23, 2016 |url-status=live |df=mdy-all }}</ref><ref name="thenorthfieldnews.com">{{cite web|url=http://www.thenorthfieldnews.com/news/2015-09-17/Front_Page/Phil_Scott_Says_Yes_at_Labor_Day_Parade_in_Northfi.html|title=Phil Scott Says Yes at Labor Day Parade in Northfield - www.thenorthfieldnews.com - Northfield News|access-date=March 3, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161222223143/http://www.thenorthfieldnews.com/news/2015-09-17/Front_Page/Phil_Scott_Says_Yes_at_Labor_Day_Parade_in_Northfi.html|archive-date=December 22, 2016|url-status=usurped}}</ref> He is a past president of the Associated [[General contractor|General Contractors]] of Vermont.<ref name="thenorthfieldnews.com"/> On January 6, 2012, a fire at DuBois Construction caused substantial damage,<ref>[http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/article/20120106/NEWS02/120106010/Building-of-construction-firm-owned-by-Vermont-Lt-Gov-Phil-Scott-aflame-in-Middlesex?odyssey=tab |topnews|text|FRONTPAGE Newspaper article, Building of Construction Firm Owned by Vermont Lieutenant Governor Burns in Middlesex]{{Dead link|date=November 2022 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, burlingtonfreepress.com, January 6, 2012.</ref><ref>[http://timesargus.com/article/20120106/THISJUSTIN/701079938 "Fire Engulfs DuBois Construction Headquarters"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120110012108/http://www.timesargus.com/article/20120106/THISJUSTIN/701079938 |date=January 10, 2012 }}, ''Barre-Montpelier Times Argus'', January 6, 2012</ref> but the owners rebuilt and continued operations.<ref>{{cite news |last=Aloe |first=Jess |date=September 24, 2016 |title=Phil Scott will sell share in business if elected |url=http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/2016/09/24/phil-scott-sell-share-business-if-elected/90687768/ |newspaper=Burlington Free Press |location=Burlington, VT}}</ref>

After being elected governor, Scott sold his share of DuBois Construction to avoid possible [[Conflict of interest|conflicts of interest]], since DuBois Construction does business with the State of Vermont. He sold his 50% share for $2.5 million plus 3% interest, payable over 15 years.{{sfn|Governor Details Construction Company Sale}} Scott said he opted to finance the sale himself rather than having the company borrow the money to pay him in full to preserve the company's bonding capacity.{{sfn|Governor Details Construction Company Sale}} Critics suggested that Scott's sale of his share in the company over 15 years did not eliminate possible conflicts of interest, but Scott and the attorney who negotiated the sale on his behalf disagreed.{{sfn|Governor Details Construction Company Sale}}

In October 2018, the state ethics commission issued an advisory opinion that Scott did have a conflict of interest because of his continued connection to the company.<ref name="Rathke2019">{{cite news |last=Rathke |first=Lisa |agency=[[Associated Press]] |date=September 5, 2019 |title=Vermont commission withdraws conflict-of-interest opinion on governor |url=https://www.vnews.com/Commission-withdraws-gov-s-conflict-of-interest-opinion-28262645 |work=[[Valley News]] |location=Lebanon, NH}}</ref> In September 2019, the commission withdrew the opinion, with its executive director saying that the process for receiving the complaint and investigating and issuing the opinion had been flawed.<ref name="Rathke2019"/> In February 2022, DuBois executives said they had reached an agreement to sell the company to Barrett Trucking of [[Burlington, Vermont|Burlington]].<ref name="Thys2022">{{cite news |last=Thys |first=Fred |date=February 14, 2022 |title=Company that owed Gov. Phil Scott $2.5 million is sold |url=https://vtdigger.org/2022/02/14/company-that-owed-gov-phil-scott-2-5-million-is-sold/ |work=VT Digger |location=Monepelier, VT}}</ref> The sale's terms were not disclosed, including whether Scott would receive a lump sum or installment payments, but DuBois representatives said the company's obligation to Scott would be met.<ref name="Thys2022"/>

== Political career ==

=== Vermont Senate ===
A [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]], Scott was elected to the [[Vermont Senate]] in 2000, one of three at-large senators representing the [[Washington County, Vermont|Washington County]] [[Washington (Vermont Senate district)|Senate district]]. He was reelected four times, and was in office from 2001 to 2011. During his Senate career, he was vice chair of the Transportation Committee and chaired the Institutions Committee. He also was a member of the Natural Resources and Energy Committee.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.leg.state.vt.us/DOCS/2002/JOURNAL/SJ010105.HTM|title=Journal of the Vermont Senate|website=leg.state.vt.us|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170207020405/http://www.leg.state.vt.us/DOCS/2002/JOURNAL/SJ010105.HTM|archive-date=February 7, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> As chair of the Institutions Committee, Scott redesigned the [[Vermont State House]] cafeteria to increase efficiency.<ref name="freep 2016 efficiency">{{cite web | title=Phil Scott: Efficiency over big ideas | website=Burlington Free Press | date=June 23, 2016 | url=https://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/2016/06/23/phil-scott-efficiency-over-big-ideas/85201644/ | access-date=October 6, 2018}}</ref>

During his time in the Senate, Scott was on several special committees, including the Judicial Nominating Board, the Legislative Advisory Committee on the State House, the Joint Oversight Corrections Committee, and the Legislative Council Committee.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://legislature.vermont.gov/people/single/2010/217|title=Senator Phil Scott|website=legislature.vermont.gov|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160601132051/http://legislature.vermont.gov/people/single/2010/217|archive-date=June 1, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

=== Lieutenant governor ===
[[File:Phil Scott.jpg|thumb|200px|Scott in 2016]]
On November 2, 2010, Scott was elected the [[List of lieutenant governors of Vermont|81st lieutenant governor of Vermont]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Phil Scott elected Vermont Lieutenant Governor|agency=Associated Press|publisher=The Burlington Free Press|date=November 3, 2010|url=http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/article/20101103/NEWS03/101103010/|access-date=November 3, 2010}}{{dead link|date=March 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> defeating [[Steven Howard (politician)|Steven Howard]]. He took office on January 6, 2011. He was reelected in 2012, defeating Cassandra Gekas, and in 2014, defeating [[Dean Corren]].

As lieutenant governor, Scott presided over the Vermont Senate during its sessions. In addition, he was a member of the Committee on Committees, the three-member panel that determines Senate committee assignments and appoints committee chairs and vice chairs. In the event of a tie vote, Scott was tasked with casting a tie-breaking vote. He also was acting governor when the governor was out of state.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://ltgov.vermont.gov/about|title=About Vermont Lieutenant Governor Phil Scott {{!}} Lieutenant Governor|website=ltgov.vermont.gov|access-date=April 30, 2016|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080905210726/http://www.ltgov.vermont.gov/about|archive-date=September 5, 2008}}</ref>

As a state senator and lieutenant governor, Scott was active with a number of community service projects. In 2005, he founded the Wheels for Warmth program, which buys used car tires and resells safe ones, with the profits going to heating fuel assistance programs in Vermont.<ref name="Keck 2015">{{cite web | last=Keck | first=Nina | title=Wheels For Warmth Comes To Rutland | website=Vermont Public Radio | date=October 26, 2015 | url=http://digital.vpr.net/post/wheels-warmth-comes-rutland | access-date=October 6, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181006234753/http://digital.vpr.net/post/wheels-warmth-comes-rutland | archive-date=October 6, 2018 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

==== Job approval as lieutenant governor====
In September 2015, Scott maintained high name recognition and favorability among Vermonters. The [[Castleton University]] Polling Institute found that more than three-quarters of Vermonters knew who he was, and that of those who were able to identify him, 70% viewed him favorably.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=http://digital.vpr.net/post/castleton-poll-gives-early-look-vermont-gubernatorial-race#stream/0|title=Castleton Poll Gives An Early Look At Vermont Gubernatorial Race|last=Hirschfeld|first=Peter|website=digital.vpr.net|date=September 21, 2015|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160630192627/http://digital.vpr.net/post/castleton-poll-gives-early-look-vermont-gubernatorial-race#stream/0|archive-date=June 30, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Despite his being a Republican, the same poll found that 59% of self-identified Democrats held a favorable view of Scott, while only 15% held an unfavorable view of him.<ref name=":2" />

==== National Lieutenant Governors Association ====
Scott was an active member of the [[National Lieutenant Governors Association]] (NLGA), and was on the NLGA's executive committee and the NLGA's finance committee.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/about-nlga/officers-executive-committee-2/|title=Officers & Executive Committee {{!}} National Lieutenant Governors Association (NLGA)|website=nlga.us|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160406050451/http://www.nlga.us/about-nlga/officers-executive-committee-2/|archive-date=April 6, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/about-nlga/operational-committees/|title=Operational Committees {{!}} National Lieutenant Governors Association (NLGA)|website=nlga.us|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160630235719/http://www.nlga.us/about-nlga/operational-committees/|archive-date=June 30, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> As a member of the NLGA, he joined fellow lieutenant governors across the country in two bipartisan letters opposing proposed cuts to the [[Army National Guard]] in 2014 and 2015.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/NLGA-National-Guard-Letter-5.1.14.pdf|title=National Lieutenant Governors Association April 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150906015506/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/NLGA-National-Guard-Letter-5.1.14.pdf|archive-date=September 6, 2015|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/041415-NLGA-Guard-Letter-Signature-FINAL.pdf|title=National Lieutenant Governors Association April 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150906042825/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/041415-NLGA-Guard-Letter-Signature-FINAL.pdf|archive-date=September 6, 2015|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott was a lead sponsor of an NLGA resolution to develop a long-term vision for surface transportation in the U.S.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/2014-NLGA-Long-Term-Transportation-Resolution-3.141.pdf|title=Resolution to Develop a Shared, Long Term Vision for Surface Transportation|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170207020056/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/2014-NLGA-Long-Term-Transportation-Resolution-3.141.pdf|archive-date=February 7, 2017|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He also co-sponsored resolutions to recognize the importance of arts and culture in tourism to the U.S. economy, to support [[Science, technology, engineering, and mathematics|Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics]] (STEM) education, to support designating a National Arts in Education Week, and to support a comprehensive system to end [[Homeless veterans in the United States|homelessness]] among U.S. veterans.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/Arts-Resolution-2014-FINAL-8.8.14-Update.pdf|title=Recognizing the Importance of Arts and Culture in Tourism to the Economy of the United States|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150906042753/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/Arts-Resolution-2014-FINAL-8.8.14-Update.pdf|archive-date=September 6, 2015|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/NLGA-STEM-Resolution-FINAL-8.8.14-Update.pdf|title=Resolution in Support of STEM Education Initiative|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150906003732/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/NLGA-STEM-Resolution-FINAL-8.8.14-Update.pdf|archive-date=September 6, 2015|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/2015-NLGA-Arts-Education-Week-Resolution-3.18.15-Updated.pdf|title=Resolution in Support of Designating the Week of September 13–19, 2015 as National Arts in Education Week|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150905183316/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/2015-NLGA-Arts-Education-Week-Resolution-3.18.15-Updated.pdf|archive-date=September 5, 2015|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/NLGA-Homeless-Vet-FINAL-update-2016.pdf|title=A Resolution In Support Of Comprehensive, Coordinated Systems to End Homelessness Among Veterans|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701004522/http://www.nlga.us/wp-content/uploads/NLGA-Homeless-Vet-FINAL-update-2016.pdf|archive-date=July 1, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

== Governor of Vermont ==

=== 2016 gubernatorial campaign===
In September 2015, Scott announced his [[2016 Vermont gubernatorial election|candidacy]] for [[Governor of Vermont|Vermont governor]].<ref>[http://www.wcax.com/story/29974334/lt-governor-phil-scott-to-run-for-vt-governor Phil Scott to run for Governor] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910020426/http://www.wcax.com/story/29974334/lt-governor-phil-scott-to-run-for-vt-governor |date=September 10, 2015 }}, wcax.com; accessed September 13, 2015.</ref>

An early 2016 poll commissioned by [[Vermont Public Radio]] and conducted by the Castleton University Polling Institute found that of the two candidates for the Republican nomination for governor, Scott was preferred by 42% of respondents compared to{{nbsp}}4% for [[Bruce Lisman]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://digital.vpr.net/post/vpr-poll-races-issues-and-full-results#stream/0|title=The VPR Poll: The Races, The Issues And The Full Results|last=Butler|first=Taylor Dobbs, Jonathan|website=digital.vpr.net|date=February 22, 2016|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160507042340/http://digital.vpr.net/post/vpr-poll-races-issues-and-full-results#stream/0|archive-date=May 7, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> A poll commissioned by Energy Independent Vermont in late June 2016 indicated that Scott had the support of 68% of Republicans to Lisman's 23%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.energyindependentvt.org/poll-vermonters-support-action-to-address-global-warming/|title=Poll: Vermonters Support Action to Address Global Warming|date=July 7, 2016|access-date=July 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818131134/http://www.energyindependentvt.org/poll-vermonters-support-action-to-address-global-warming/|archive-date=August 18, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

On May 8, 2016, Scott was endorsed by nearly all of Vermont's Republican legislators.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.vermontbiz.com/news/may/republican-legislators-endorse-phil-scott-governor|title=Republican legislators endorse Phil Scott for governor {{!}} Vermont Business Magazine|website=vermontbiz.com|access-date=May 5, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160506163539/http://www.vermontbiz.com/news/may/republican-legislators-endorse-phil-scott-governor|archive-date=May 6, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He did not support [[Donald Trump]] in the 2016 presidential campaign.<ref>{{cite web | last=Midura | first=Kyle | title=Vt. candidates for governor debate in Burlington | website=WCAX.COM Local Vermont News, Weather and Sports | date=May 13, 2016 | url=http://www.wcax.com/story/31970447/vt-candidates-for-governor-debate-in-burlington | access-date=May 21, 2016 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160518033914/http://www.wcax.com/story/31970447/vt-candidates-for-governor-debate-in-burlington | archive-date=May 18, 2016 | url-status=dead | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

On August 9, Scott defeated Lisman in the primary election by 21 percentage points.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/2016/08/09/phil-scott-claims-gop-primary-victory-governor/88292810/|title=Phil Scott claims GOP primary victory for governor|work=Burlington Free Press|access-date=April 18, 2017|language=en}}</ref> He defeated [[Sue Minter]], the Democratic nominee, in the November general election by 8.7 percentage points.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/2016/08/09/sue-minter-wins-democratic-nomination-governor/88292776/|title=Sue Minter wins Democratic nomination for governor|first=Jess|last=Aloe|work=Burlington Free Press|date=August 9, 2016|access-date=August 10, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/2016-election/results/map/governor/vermont/|title=Vermont Election Results 2016: Governor Live Map by County, Real-Time Voting Updates|website=Election Hub|access-date=April 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170418163418/http://www.politico.com/2016-election/results/map/governor/vermont/|archive-date=April 18, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

=== Governorship ===

[[File:Phil Scott 170309-Z-CD688-205 (32509129154).jpg|thumb|left|upright|Scott in 2017]]

==== Fraud case settlement ====
On April 13, 2017, Scott announced a $150 million settlement with Raymond James Financial, Inc. as part of resolving fraud allegations by contractors and investors related to the [[Jay Peak Resort|Jay Peak]] and [[Burke Mountain Ski Area|Burke Mountain]] [[EB-5 visa|EB-5]] developments.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/150-million-settlement-reached-reimburse-jay-peak-and-burke-mountain-creditors|title=$150 Million Settlement Reached to Reimburse Jay Peak and Burke Mountain Creditors|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=April 18, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170419002658/http://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/150-million-settlement-reached-reimburse-jay-peak-and-burke-mountain-creditors|archive-date=April 19, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

==== Job approval ====
According to an October 2017 [[Morning Consult]] poll, Scott's approval rating stood at 60%, making him the 7th most popular governor in the country. The poll was conducted between July 1 and September 30, 2017, and had a margin of error of 4%. In April 2018, another Morning Consult poll found that Scott's approval rating had risen to 65%, making him the 4th most popular governor in the country.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://morningconsult.com/2017/10/31/americas-most-least-popular-governors-october-2017/|title=America's Most and Least Popular Governors|date=October 31, 2017|work=Morning Consult|access-date=November 14, 2017|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171114093214/https://morningconsult.com/2017/10/31/americas-most-least-popular-governors-october-2017/|archive-date=November 14, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> His favorability ratings fell to 52% by May 2018,<ref>{{cite web |website=vtdigger.com |url=https://vtdigger.org/2018/06/11/scott-polling-almost-20-lower-2016-election/ |title=Scott polling almost 20% lower than before 2016 election |author=Colin Meyn |date=June 11, 2018 |access-date=October 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181006195357/https://vtdigger.org/2018/06/11/scott-polling-almost-20-lower-2016-election/ |archive-date=October 6, 2018 |url-status=live |df=mdy-all }}</ref> and to 47% by July, marking the largest decrease in popularity for any governor in the nation.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://morningconsult.com/2018/07/25/americas-most-and-least-popular-governors-2/|title=America's Most and Least Popular Governors -- July 2018|date=July 25, 2018|work=Morning Consult|access-date=September 16, 2018|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180915215659/https://morningconsult.com/2018/07/25/americas-most-and-least-popular-governors-2/|archive-date=September 15, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> By April 2019, Scott's approval rating had recovered to 59%, with a 28% disapproval rating, making him the 5th most popular governor in the country, with a net approval of 31%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://morningconsult.com/governor-rankings/|title=Morning Consult's Governor Approval Rankings|website=Morning Consult|language=en-US|access-date=2019-07-02|archive-date=February 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200206122506/https://morningconsult.com/governor-rankings/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

== Political positions ==
Scott has been called a [[Moderate wing of the Republican Party (United States)|moderate]],<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usnews.com/news/politics/articles/2021-08-23/how-republican-gov-phil-scott-won-over-blue-vermont|title=Blue, But Bipartisan: Can Vermont Be a Model for the Rest of the U.S.? |work=U.S. News and World Report|last=Jacobson |first=Louis |date=August 23, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wcax.com/2024/05/15/gov-scott-aims-be-voice-moderate-vermonters-he-navigates-party-trump/|title=Gov. Scott aims to be 'voice' of moderate Vermonters as he navigates party of Trump|website=WCAX.com|last=Cutler|first=Calvin|date=May 15, 2024}}</ref> as well as a [[Liberal wing of the Republican Party (United States)|liberal Republican]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2018/11/republican-governors-new-england-defy-blue-wave/574726/|title=The Last Liberal Republicans Hang On|last=Richards|first=Parker|date=November 3, 2018|work=The Atlantic|access-date=November 9, 2018|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181109112034/https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2018/11/republican-governors-new-england-defy-blue-wave/574726/|archive-date=November 9, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> His views are "[[Fiscal conservatism|fiscally conservative]] but [[Cultural liberalism|socially liberal]]".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/nation/politics/trailguide/la-na-election-day-2016-republican-phil-scott-wins-vermont-1478666719-htmlstory.html|title=Republican Phil Scott wins Vermont governor's race|last=King|first=Laura|website=Los Angeles Times|date=November 10, 2016 |access-date=July 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704035430/http://www.latimes.com/nation/politics/trailguide/la-na-election-day-2016-republican-phil-scott-wins-vermont-1478666719-htmlstory.html|archive-date=July 4, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://apnews.com/article/republican-governor-phil-scott-vermont-election-f73b13e44d24697ee21419988ed2351a|title=Republican Vermont Gov. Phil Scott is running for reelection to 5th term |work=Associated Press|date=May 12, 2024}}</ref> Scott has said: "I am very much a fiscal conservative. But not unlike most Republicans in the Northeast, I'm probably more on the left of center from a social standpoint{{nbsp}}... I am a pro-choice Republican".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2016/12/25/governors-lead-a-republican-renaissance-in-new-england/|title=Governors lead a Republican renaissance in New England|date=December 25, 2016|work=Press Herald|access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704035447/https://www.pressherald.com/2016/12/25/governors-lead-a-republican-renaissance-in-new-england/|archive-date=July 4, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He supported the [[impeachment inquiry into Donald Trump]] that began in September 2019,<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/a-first-vermont-gop-governor-backs-trump-impeachment-probe/2019/09/26/c2f22732-e075-11e9-be7f-4cc85017c36f_story.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190926170805/https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/a-first-vermont-gop-governor-backs-trump-impeachment-probe/2019/09/26/c2f22732-e075-11e9-be7f-4cc85017c36f_story.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=2019-09-26|title=A first: Vermont GOP governor backs Trump impeachment probe|agency=Associated Press|newspaper=Washington Post|language=en|access-date=2019-09-26}}</ref> and called for Trump to "resign or be removed from office" after the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack|2021 storming of the U.S. Capitol building]].<ref name="Trump resign">{{cite news |last1=Sukiennik |first1=Greg |title=Gov. Phil Scott calls on Trump to 'resign or be removed' |url=https://www.reformer.com/local-news/gov-phil-scott-calls-on-trump-to-resign-or-be-removed/article_e9dc9efa-5071-11eb-95a1-ebad28fcf6c6.html |access-date=7 January 2021 |work=[[Brattleboro Reformer]] |date=January 6, 2021 |language=en}}</ref> In the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020]] and [[2024 United States presidential election|2024]] presidential elections, Scott announced he voted for Democratic nominees [[Joe Biden]] and [[Kamala Harris]], respectively,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mastrangelo|first=Dominick|date=2020-11-03|title=Vermont's GOP governor says he voted for Biden|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/524307-vermonts-gop-governor-says-he-voted-for-biden|access-date=2020-11-16|website=TheHill|language=en}}</ref><ref name="Harris vote">{{cite web |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/4974107-vermonts-gop-governor-votes-kamala-harris/ |title=Vermont's GOP governor votes for Harris: 'I had to put country over party' |last=Timotija |first=Filip |website=The Hill |date=November 5, 2024 |accessdate=November 8, 2024 }}</ref> calling the latter "a vote against Donald Trump" and a move to "put country over party".<ref name="Harris vote"/>

=== Fiscal and budgetary issues ===
Scott pledged to veto any budget that grows faster than the [[Economic growth|growth rate]] of the underlying economy or wages in the previous year, or that increases statewide property taxes. Conflicts over raising property tax rates, which the state legislature supported and Scott opposed, led to a strained relationship between him and the legislature in 2018 for the FY19 budget, despite high revenues overall.<ref name="Hirschfeld 2018">{{cite web | last=Hirschfeld | first=Peter | title=As Lawmakers Close Out 2018 Session, Scott Vows Budget Veto | website=Vermont Public Radio | date=May 12, 2018 | url=http://digital.vpr.net/post/lawmakers-close-out-2018-session-scott-vows-budget-veto | access-date=October 6, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181006195301/http://digital.vpr.net/post/lawmakers-close-out-2018-session-scott-vows-budget-veto | archive-date=October 6, 2018 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

Scott made addressing Vermont's long-term unfunded liabilities a priority, and worked with State Treasurer [[Beth Pearce]] to pay down Vermont's pension debt.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mynbc5.com/article/new-vermont-state-budget-signed-into-law/28104385|title=New Vermont state budget signed into law|date=2019-06-19|website=WPTZ|language=en|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref>

=== Taxes and fees ===
The FY18 budget Scott signed into law did not include any new or increased taxes or fees. He has said that he opposes any new taxes.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.mychamplainvalley.com/news/governor-phil-scott-outlines-2019-budget-calls-on-legislature-to-pass-budget-with-no-new-taxes/935544461|title=Governor Phil Scott outlines 2019 budget, calls on legislature to pass budget with no new taxes|last=Conlin|first=Spencer|date=January 24, 2018|work=MYCHAMPLAINVALLEY|access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704034959/https://www.mychamplainvalley.com/news/governor-phil-scott-outlines-2019-budget-calls-on-legislature-to-pass-budget-with-no-new-taxes/935544461|archive-date=July 4, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He also refused to sign a bill that would have raised property taxes.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/government/2018/05/30/vermont-tax-standoff-how-lawmakers-and-gov-scott-got-impasse/644857002/|title=Vermont tax standoff: How Gov. Scott and lawmakers got to this impasse|work=Burlington Free Press|access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref> Scott vetoed the FY19 budget twice before allowing it to go into law without his signature, as the threat of a government shutdown approached.<ref name="PHirschfeld 2018">{{cite web | last=Hirschfeld | first=Peter | title=Scott Will Allow Budget To Pass Without His Signature | website=Vermont Public Radio | date=June 26, 2018 | url=http://digital.vpr.net/post/scott-will-allow-budget-pass-without-his-signature | access-date=October 11, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181012014628/http://digital.vpr.net/post/scott-will-allow-budget-pass-without-his-signature | archive-date=October 12, 2018 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref><ref name="Jordan 2018">{{cite web | last=Jordan | first=David | title=Budget bill will become law without governor's signature | website=AP NEWS | date=June 26, 2018 | url=https://apnews.com/310b92103c4b42e284c50df3695c6ec0 | access-date=October 11, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181012053956/https://apnews.com/310b92103c4b42e284c50df3695c6ec0 | archive-date=October 12, 2018 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

In early 2018, Scott called for eliminating the tax on [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]] benefits. House legislators incorporated a modified form of this proposal into the final FY19 budget, eliminating the tax for low- and middle-income retirees.<ref>{{cite news|author=Xander Landen|date=July 30, 2018|title=Social Security income tax exemptions are now in place|url=https://vtdigger.org/2018/07/30/social-security-income-tax-exemptions-are-now-in-place/|work=VT Digger|access-date=October 11, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181012055414/https://vtdigger.org/2018/07/30/social-security-income-tax-exemptions-are-now-in-place/|archive-date=October 12, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> The tax reform Scott planned (which was ultimately implemented) also lowered state income tax rates by 0.2% for all brackets; tied Vermont's tax system to Adjusted Gross Income (AGI); created Vermont-defined income deductions and personal exemptions similar to the federal tax code; increased the state [[earned income tax credit]] by three percentage points; and added a new 5% charitable contribution tax credit.<ref name="governor.vermont.gov">{{Cite web|url=https://governor.vermont.gov/content/fiscal-discipline-responsible-tax-relief|title=FISCAL DISCIPLINE & RESPONSIBLE TAX RELIEF {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref> Scott's administration has reduced both Workers' Compensation and Unemployment Insurance tax rates.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://governor.vermont.gov/content/reducing-costs-families-businesses|title=REDUCING COSTS FOR FAMILIES & BUSINESSES {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref> He has twice proposed to phase out the tax on military retirement income, which the legislature did not advance.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/government/2018/01/23/gov-scott-budget-calls-tax-exemption-social-security-income/1014119001/|title=Gov. Scott calls for tax exemption on Social Security income|website=Burlington Free Press|language=en|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://vtdigger.org/2019/01/24/scott-changes-course-taxes-fees-budget-address/|title=Scott changes course on taxes and fees in budget address|first1=Anne|last1=Galloway|first2=Xander|last2=Landen|date=January 24, 2019|website=VTDigger|language=en-US|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref>

=== Economic development ===
In 2016, Scott set a goal of boosting the state's economy by increasing the state's population to 700,000 within 10 years by encouraging young people who come to study in the state to remain after graduation.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://vtdigger.org/2016/02/21/margolis-slow-population-growth-might-be-the-norm-in-vermont/|title=Margolis: VT's slow population growth might be the norm|date=February 21, 2016|website=VTDigger|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160603072704/http://vtdigger.org/2016/02/21/margolis-slow-population-growth-might-be-the-norm-in-vermont/|archive-date=June 3, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> [[University of Vermont]] economics professor Arthur Woolf Scott suggested that retention of older Vermonters, with larger incomes and tax revenues, would be a better focus, but Scott pointed to the lower average healthcare costs associated with a younger population.<ref name="Isaacs 2017">{{cite web | last=Isaacs | first=Abby | title=Phil Scott's economic plan looks to encourage young people to stay in Vermont | website=WPTZ | date=October 8, 2017 | url=https://www.mynbc5.com/en/article/untitled-content-1474052383/3389569 | access-date=October 11, 2018 }}{{Dead link|date=January 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

Scott's economic development plan has largely focused on workforce development and economic incentives. He has advocated and achieved increasing tax credits for development, new support for small business, additional initiatives for rural economic growth, [[tax increment financing]], permitting reform, and tax exemptions in key industries.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://governor.vermont.gov/content/economic-expansion|title=Economic Expansion {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref> Scott has made expanding the labor force a priority of his administration, and has proposed and achieved initiatives that invest in workforce recruitment, retention, and relocation.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://governor.vermont.gov/content/expanding-strengthening-vermont%E2%80%99s-workforce|title=EXPANDING & STRENGTHENING VERMONT'S WORKFORCE {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref>

=== Healthcare ===
Scott signed a bill requiring Vermonters to have health insurance, making Vermont among a few states to implement such a policy after the federal repeal of the [[Individual shared responsibility provision|individual mandate provision]] of the [[Affordable Care Act]].<ref name="Ring 2018">{{cite web | last=Ring | first=Wilson | title=Vermont to require that all have health insurance | website=The Seattle Times | date=June 10, 2018 | url=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/vermont-to-require-that-all-have-health-insurance/ | access-date=October 11, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181012014633/https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/vermont-to-require-that-all-have-health-insurance/ | archive-date=October 12, 2018 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> But in part due to his opposition to a financial penalty for an individual mandate, the legislature passed and Scott signed a bill that would simply require attestation of health insurance.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://legislature.vermont.gov/bill/status/2020/H.524|title=Bill Status H.524 (Act 63)|website=legislature.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref>

Scott has advocated moving away from a fee-for-service-based healthcare system, and has suggested focusing more on the quality of care and services rendered. This model has been implemented on a pilot basis with an [[accountable care organization]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://governor.vermont.gov/content/supporting-safe-and-healthy-communities|title=Supporting Safe and Healthy Communities {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref>

In April 2021, [[Bryan Kehl]] and [[Christopher Rufo]], among others, criticized Scott for implementing a race-based [[COVID-19 vaccine|COVID-19 vaccination]] schedule.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klein|first=Melissa|date=April 3, 2021|title=Vermont Gov. Phil Scott slammed by critics, ex-Giant for race-based vax schedule|url=https://nypost.com/2021/04/03/vermont-gov-phil-scott-slammed-by-critics-for-race-based-vax-schedule/|website=New York Post}}</ref> In response, he released a statement condemning what he called a "racist response" to the plan.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/vermont/articles/2021-04-05/vermont-opening-covid-19-vaccines-to-those-40-and-older|website=US News|title=Scott Decries 'Racist Response' to BIPOC Vaccine Eligibility|accessdate=2021-04-11|date=April 5, 2021}}</ref>

=== Education ===
Scott has called for modifying Act 46 to improve cost containment measures, incorporate property tax reduction, preserve local control and school choice, and allow communities to keep the funds they save through school district mergers.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=http://www.philscott.org/issues/making-vermont-more-affordable/|title=Making Vermont More Affordable – Phil Scott For Governor|website=Phil Scott For Governor|language=en-US|access-date=April 29, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160421072852/http://www.philscott.org/issues/making-vermont-more-affordable/|archive-date=April 21, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He has expressed support for flexible learning plans and new technologies to improve educational outcomes.<ref name=":0" />

Scott's FY18 budget made investments in education, including a $3 million increase in the base appropriation to the [[Vermont State Colleges]] to stabilize tuition and a new position in the [[Vermont Agency of Education|Agency of Education]] focused on career and technical education.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/governor-scotts-blog/foundation-growth|title=A Foundation for Growth {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=November 14, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171114145101/http://governor.vermont.gov/governor-scotts-blog/foundation-growth|archive-date=November 14, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> The budget also expanded a base appropriation for child care financial assistance by $2.5 million.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/expanding-strengthening-vermont%E2%80%99s-workforce|title=EXPANDING & STRENGTHENING VERMONT'S WORKFORCE {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=September 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916235611/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/expanding-strengthening-vermont%E2%80%99s-workforce|archive-date=September 16, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> The FY20 budget Scott signed into law built on these investments, with an additional $7.4 million for child care and $3 million more for higher education.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ljfo.vermont.gov/assets/Uploads/e23370a8bb/H_542_FY_2020_Budget_Highlight_Doc.pdf|title=FY20 Conference Committee Budget Highlights|last=JFO|date=May 2019|website=ljfo.vermont.gov}}</ref> The next year, Scott worked with the legislature to eliminate tuition for members of the [[Vermont National Guard]].<ref name=":4" />

As a state senator, Scott voted for legislation to reduce education property tax rates.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.leg.state.vt.us/database/status/rcdetail.cfm?Session=2006&RollCallID=3458|title=The Vermont Legislative Bill Tracking System|website=leg.state.vt.us|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160509182751/http://www.leg.state.vt.us/database/status/rcdetail.cfm?Session=2006&RollCallID=3458|archive-date=May 9, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.leg.state.vt.us/docs/legdoc.cfm?URL=/docs/2006/acts/ACT001SUM.HTM|title=Legislative Documents|website=leg.state.vt.us|access-date=April 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160512201728/http://www.leg.state.vt.us/docs/legdoc.cfm?URL=%2Fdocs%2F2006%2Facts%2FACT001SUM.HTM|archive-date=May 12, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott's FY18 budget froze property tax rates, and the FY19 budget froze residential property tax rates.<ref name=":9" /><ref name="governor.vermont.gov"/>

In July 2025, Scott signed an education reform bill, marking a transitional shift in the Vermont public school system.<ref>{{cite web|title=Ceremonial signing puts transformational Vermont education reform bill into law|url=https://www.mynbc5.com/article/vermont-education-reform-bill-law/65270089|date=2025-07-01|website=mynbc5.com}}</ref>

=== Gun law ===
Scott passed legislation that banned bump stock devices, expanded background checks for gun purchases, raised the age to purchase firearms to 21 (with certain exceptions), limited the sale of certain high-capacity magazines, increased restrictions on the sale of firearms to alleged domestic abusers, and created risk protection orders.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/official-statement-s55-s221-h422|title=OFFICIAL STATEMENT ON S.55, S.221 & H.422 {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=September 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916235522/http://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/official-statement-s55-s221-h422|archive-date=September 16, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> In September 2018, Scott created a Violence Prevention Task Force, ordered a security assessment of all Vermont schools, and signed legislation appropriating $5 million for school security grants.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/supporting-safe-and-healthy-communities|title=Supporting Safe and Healthy Communities {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=September 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180917034131/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/supporting-safe-and-healthy-communities|archive-date=September 17, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott also signed gun control legislation that "limits some aspects of gun possession and empowers authorities to remove guns from people who may be dangerous."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/story/news/politics/government/2018/04/11/vermont-gun-bills-governor-phil-scott-signs-laws-amid-protest/494166002/|title=Gov. Scott signs VT gun bills, calling for civility, as protesters yell 'traitor'|work=Burlington Free Press|access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref>

=== Government reform and modernization ===
Scott supports limiting Vermont's annual legislative session to 90 days. According to him, the session's unpredictable length discourages everyday Vermonters from running for office.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|url=http://www.philscott.org/press-release/phil-scott-announces-new-legislative-budget-initiatives/|title=Phil Scott Announces New Legislative and Budget Initiatives – Phil Scott For Governor|date=May 10, 2016|website=Phil Scott For Governor|language=en-US|access-date=May 13, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160628200307/http://www.philscott.org/press-release/phil-scott-announces-new-legislative-budget-initiatives/|archive-date=June 28, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> A 90-day session, according to Scott, would encourage more people to run for elected office by setting clear parameters.<ref name=":6" /> Furthermore, he argues that a 90-day session would force the legislature to focus on key fiscal and operational issues.<ref name=":6" />

As governor, Scott created a Government Modernization and Efficiency Team to implement efficiency audits, strengthen IT planning, implement a digital government strategy, and identify opportunities to eliminate inefficiencies, establish clear metrics and streamline services.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/governors-government-modernization-and-efficiency-team-executive-order-03-17|title=Governor's Government Modernization and Efficiency Team (Executive Order 03-17) {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=November 14, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171114093504/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/governors-government-modernization-and-efficiency-team-executive-order-03-17|archive-date=November 14, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He also created the Program to Improve Vermont Outcomes Together (PIVOT) initiative, which asks frontline state employees for ways to make state government systems more efficient and easier to use.<ref name=":9" /> Scott consolidated IT functions in state government with the creation of the Agency of Digital Services, saving $2.19 million.<ref name=":9" /><ref name=":5">{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/modernizing-state-government|title=Modernizing State Government {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=September 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916235642/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/modernizing-state-government|archive-date=September 16, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He also merged the Department of Liquor Control and the Lottery Department into the Department of Liquor and Lottery to achieve savings.<ref name=":5" /> Scott's administration has worked to achieve internal improvements through lean training and permit process improvements.<ref name=":5" /> He also successfully sought to eliminate and merge redundant boards, commissions, studies and reports.<ref name=":5" />

=== Transportation ===
In July 2016, Scott outlined the transportation priorities he would implement as governor.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.philscott.org/news/phil-scott-vermont-transportation-priorities/|title=Phil Scott for Vermont Transportation Priorities – Phil Scott For Governor|date=July 28, 2016|language=en-US|access-date=August 1, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160805203322/http://www.philscott.org/news/phil-scott-vermont-transportation-priorities/|archive-date=August 5, 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> He said he would strengthen the link between economic growth and Vermont's infrastructure; oppose additional transportation taxes, including a [[carbon tax]]; oppose accumulating additional state debt for transportation; encourage innovation in transportation by implementing a [[Research and development|Research and Development]] (R&D) tax credit and an Angel Investor tax credit (a 60% credit toward cash equity investments in Vermont businesses, specifically targeted toward transportation, energy and manufacturing firms); protect the state's transportation fund to ensure it is used for transportation purposes only; advocate federal reforms and flexibility in transportation policy; and update the [[Vermont Agency of Transportation|Agency of Transportation's]] long-range plan for transportation.

=== Abortion ===

Scott is [[Pro-choice and pro-life#Pro-choice|pro-choice]]. In June 2019, he signed into law an [[United States abortion-rights movement|abortion rights]] bill that was among the most wide-ranging in the U.S. in providing for abortion at any time, protecting "the fundamental right of every individual who becomes pregnant to choose to carry a pregnancy to term, to give birth to a child, or to have an abortion."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wcax.com/content/news/Gov-Scott-signs-bill-that-preserves-right-to-an-abortion-511112282.html|title=Scott signs bill that preserves right to an abortion|last=WCAX|website=wcax.com|date=June 11, 2019 |language=en|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref> In June 2022, Scott expressed his disappointment over the [[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization|overturning of Roe v. Wade]], and announced that a constitutional amendment to safeguard abortion rights would appear on the November ballot. In December 2022, he signed a constitutional amendment passed by Vermont voters to further protect the right to abortion in the state.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-12-13 |title=Vermont governor signs amendment protecting abortion rights |url=https://apnews.com/article/abortion-health-jim-condos-phil-scott-government-and-politics-aa6fbc08e857c67cb624e3da40842e10 |access-date=2024-01-05 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

=== LGBT ===

Scott supports [[same-sex marriage]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wptz.com/news/vermont-new-york/burlington/In-liberal-Vt-Republican-Lt-Gov-manages-win/17317148|title=In liberal Vt., Republican Lt. Gov. manages win|last=Thurston|first=Jack|website=WPTZ|date=November 7, 2012|access-date=April 29, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160601143549/http://www.wptz.com/news/vermont-new-york/burlington/In-liberal-Vt-Republican-Lt-Gov-manages-win/17317148|archive-date=June 1, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He signed into law a gender-neutral bathroom bill intended to recognize the rights of transgender people.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.christianpost.com/news/republican-governor-phil-scott-signs-vermont-gender-neutral-bathroom-bill-law-223975/|title=Republican Gov. Phil Scott Signs Vermont Gender-Neutral Bathroom Bill Into Law|access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704035456/https://www.christianpost.com/news/republican-governor-phil-scott-signs-vermont-gender-neutral-bathroom-bill-law-223975/|archive-date=July 4, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Of the new law, he said, "Vermont has a well-earned reputation for embracing equality and being inclusive".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/05/13/us/vermont-gender-neutral-restroom-bill/index.html|title=Vermont passes gender-neutral bathroom bill|first=Kwegyirba |last=Croffie|work=CNN|access-date=July 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180629160618/https://www.cnn.com/2018/05/13/us/vermont-gender-neutral-restroom-bill/index.html|archive-date=June 29, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

=== Drugs ===
[[File:Terri Sewell - U.S. House Ways & Means Subcommittee on Health Opioid Hearing - 2018.ogg|thumb|Scott being questioned by [[Terri Sewell]] while testifying in Congress about the opioid epidemic in 2018]]
On May 24, 2017, Scott vetoed a bill that would have [[Cannabis in Vermont|legalized recreational marijuana]] in Vermont.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://money.cnn.com/2017/05/24/news/vermont-marijuana-legalization/index.html|title=Vermont governor rejects recreational pot bill|last=Smith|first=Aaron|date=May 24, 2017|website=CNNMoney|access-date=May 25, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170524175808/http://money.cnn.com/2017/05/24/news/vermont-marijuana-legalization/index.html|archive-date=May 24, 2017|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> In October 2020, he announced he would not veto another bill to legalize recreational marijuana use, allowing the bill to become a law without his signature.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Frias|first=Nikki|title='More Work Is Needed' Says Gov. Phil Scott After Vermont Legalizes Recreational Cannabis|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/nikkifrias/2020/10/11/more-work-is-needed-says-gov-phil-scott-after-vermont-legalizes-recreational-cannabis/|access-date=2020-10-19|website=Forbes|language=en}}</ref>

As governor, Scott created an Opioid Coordination Council, appointed a director of drug policy and prevention, and convened a statewide summit on growing the workforce to support opioid and substance abuse treatment.<ref name=":9" /> To further treatment options, he worked with the Secretary of State's Office of Professional Regulation to streamline the licensing process for treatment professionals.<ref name=":12">{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/addressing-opioid-epidemic|title=ADDRESSING THE OPIOID EPIDEMIC {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=September 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916235426/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/addressing-opioid-epidemic|archive-date=September 16, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott boosted efforts to reduce the drug supply through the Vermont Drug Task Force, Drug Take Back days, and expanding prescription drug disposal sites.<ref name=":12" />

===Immigration===
Scott opposed the [[Immigration policy of the first Donald Trump administration|Trump administration's immigration policies]]. In 2017, he signed a bill to limit the involvement of Vermont police with the federal government regarding immigration,<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.mynbc5.com/article/vermont-governor-signs-law-countering-trump-immigration-plan/9197740|title=Gov. Scott signs Vermont law countering Trump immigration plan|agency=Associated Press|date=March 28, 2017|work=WPTZ|access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704063526/http://www.mynbc5.com/article/vermont-governor-signs-law-countering-trump-immigration-plan/9197740|archive-date=July 4, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> and the Department of Justice notified Vermont that it had been preliminarily found to be a [[Sanctuary city|sanctuary jurisdiction]] on November 15, 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-sends-letters-29-jurisdictions-regarding-their-compliance-8-usc-1373|publisher=[[United States Department of Justice]]|date=November 15, 2017|access-date=August 2, 2018|title=Justice Department Sends Letters to 29 Jurisdictions Regarding Their Compliance with 8 U.S.C. 1373|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180801064259/https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-sends-letters-29-jurisdictions-regarding-their-compliance-8-usc-1373|archive-date=August 1, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott opposed the Trump administration's "zero tolerance" policy and the [[Trump administration family separation policy|separation of families]] at the border.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/19/us/national-guard-trump-children-immigration.html|title=Governors Refuse to Send National Guard to Border, Citing Child Separation Practice|work=The New York Times |date=June 19, 2018 |access-date=July 3, 2018|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180626080031/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/19/us/national-guard-trump-children-immigration.html|archive-date=June 26, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all|last1=Haag |first1=Matthew |last2=Bidgood |first2=Jess }}</ref>

=== Environmental issues ===
Scott approved $48 million for clean water funding in 2017.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/sites/scott/files/documents/Protecting%20the%20Vulnerable_0.pdf|title=Protecting the Vulnerable|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181204151700/https://governor.vermont.gov/sites/scott/files/documents/Protecting%20the%20Vulnerable_0.pdf|archive-date=December 4, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He signed an executive order creating the Vermont Climate Action Commission.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/vermont-climate-action-commission-executive-order-12-17|title=Vermont Climate Action Commission (Executive Order 12-17) {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=November 14, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171114093559/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/vermont-climate-action-commission-executive-order-12-17|archive-date=November 14, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott announced a settlement with [[Saint-Gobain]] to address water quality issues and [[Perfluorooctanoic acid|PFOA]] contamination in [[Bennington County, Vermont|Bennington County]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/governor-phil-scott-attorney-general-tj-donovan-and-bennington-county-legislative|title=Governor Phil Scott, Attorney General TJ Donovan and Bennington County Legislative Leaders Announce a Settlement with Saint-Gobain {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=November 14, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171114093600/http://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/governor-phil-scott-attorney-general-tj-donovan-and-bennington-county-legislative|archive-date=November 14, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> His FY18 budget proposal called for a tax holiday on energy efficient products and vehicles.<ref name=":8">{{Cite web|url=http://www.leg.state.vt.us/jfo/appropriations/fy_2018/FY2018_Executive_Budget_Summary.pdf|title=FY 2018 Executive Budget Summary|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170418164848/http://www.leg.state.vt.us/jfo/appropriations/fy_2018/FY2018_Executive_Budget_Summary.pdf|archive-date=April 18, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> On June 2, 2017, Scott led Vermont to join the [[United States Climate Alliance]], after [[President of the United States|President]] [[Donald Trump|Trump]] [[United States withdrawal from the Paris Agreement|withdrew]] the U.S. from the [[Paris Agreement]].<ref name="USCA">{{cite news|url=https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/vermont/articles/2017-06-02/vermont-to-join-us-climate-alliance|title=Vermont to Join US Climate Alliance|date=June 2, 2017|work=US News|access-date=June 2, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170603052830/https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/vermont/articles/2017-06-02/vermont-to-join-us-climate-alliance|archive-date=June 3, 2017|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Scott committed to achieving 90% renewable energy by 2050.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://governor.vermont.gov/content/preserving-environment|title=Preserving the Environment {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|language=en|access-date=September 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916235309/http://governor.vermont.gov/content/preserving-environment|archive-date=September 16, 2018|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref> In 2019, he signed several pieces of legislation related to water quality, including creating a long-term funding mechanism for cleaning up the state's waterways, testing for lead in schools and child care centers, and regulating [[perfluorooctanoic acid]] and related PFAS chemicals in drinking water.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://legislature.vermont.gov/bill/status/2020/S.96|title=Bill Status S.96 (Act 76)|website=legislature.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://governor.vermont.gov/press-release/governor-phil-scott-signs-lead-testing-remediation-bill-law|title=Governor Phil Scott Signs Lead Testing & Remediation Bill Into Law {{!}} Office of Governor Phil Scott|website=governor.vermont.gov|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.vpirg.org/news/governor-signs-pfas-protection-bill/|title=Governor Signs PFAS Protection Bill|date=2019-05-16|website=Vermont Public Interest Research Group|language=en-US|access-date=2019-07-02}}</ref> On September 15, 2020, Scott vetoed the Global Warming Solutions Act, which mandated reductions to Vermont's carbon emissions.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Norton |first1=Kit |title=Scott Vetoes Global Warming Solutions Act |url=https://vtdigger.org/2020/09/15/scott-vetoes-global-warming-solutions-act-%EF%BB%BF/ |access-date=26 October 2020 |agency=VT Digger |publisher=VT Digger |date=15 September 2020}}</ref> Ten days later, his veto was overridden.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McDermott |first1=Maeve |title=Vermont passes Global Warming Solutions Act, overriding governor's veto |url=https://www.jurist.org/news/2020/09/vermont-passes-global-solutions-act-overriding-governors-veto/ |access-date=26 October 2020 |agency=Jurist |publisher=Jurist |date=25 September 2020}}</ref>

=== 2025 deployment of National Guard in U.S. cities ===
In 2025, the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]] initiated federal deployments of [[National Guard (United States)|National Guard]] and other government entities to several U.S. cities, citing aims to combat crime, civil unrest, and threats to federal property. In August 2025, Scott declined a federal request to deploy Vermont's National Guard to Washington, D.C., as part of the administration's expanded security operations in that city.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Adams |first=Destinee |date=2025-08-20 |title=Former National Guard vice chief calls deployment to D.C. an 'intimidation' tactic |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/08/20/nx-s1-5507032/more-national-guard-deployed-to-washington-dc |access-date=2025-10-14 |work=NPR |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hirschfeld |first=Peter |date=2025-08-15 |title=Phil Scott rejects second request to deploy Vermont National Guard, this time to Washington, D.C. |url=https://www.vermontpublic.org/local-news/2025-08-15/phil-scott-rejects-request-deploy-vermont-national-guard-washington-d-c |access-date=2025-10-14 |website=Vermont Public |language=en}}</ref>

In October 2025, Scott called Trump's decision to dispatch the National Guard to cities like [[Chicago]] and [[Portland, Oregon|Portland]] "unconstitutional",<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robinson |first=Shaun |date=2025-10-09 |title=Gov. Phil Scott says Trump’s national guard deployments are unconstitutional |url=https://vtdigger.org/2025/10/09/gov-phil-scott-says-trumps-national-guard-deployments-are-unconstitutional/ |access-date=2025-10-14 |website=VTDigger |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cutler |first=Calvin |date=2025-10-10 |title=Trump’s National Guard deployment ‘unconstitutional,’ Gov. Scott says |url=https://www.wcax.com/2025/10/10/trumps-national-guard-deployment-unconstitutional-gov-scott-says/ |access-date=2025-10-14 |website=www.wcax.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rossi |first=Isabella |date=2025-10-13 |title=Guarding the Constitution: Vermont’s Phil Scott Confronts Federal Overreach - The Dartmouth Independent |url=https://dartmouthindependent.com/campus/2025/10/scott-constitutional-guard-deployment |access-date=2025-10-14 |language=en-US}}</ref> arguing that militarizing domestic settings without clear justification threatens public stability and encroaches on state sovereignty.

== Racing career ==
Scott is a champion [[stock car racing|stock car]] [[Auto racing|racer]].<ref name="thenorthfieldnews.com"/> He won the 1996 and 1998 [[Thunder Road International SpeedBowl|Thunder Road]] Late Model Series (LMS) championships and the 1997 and 1999 Thunder Road Milk Bowls.<ref name="thenorthfieldnews.com"/> (The Milk Bowl is Thunder Road's annual season finale.)<ref name="thenorthfieldnews.com"/>

In 2002, he became a three-time champion, winning both the Thunder Road and Airborne Late Model Series track championships and the [[American Canadian Tour]] championship.<ref name="thenorthfieldnews.com"/> (Airborne Park Speedway is a stock car track in the town of [[Plattsburgh (town), New York|Plattsburgh, New York]]).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.airborneparkspeedway.com/ |title=Home Page |work=Airborne Park Speedway |location=Plattsburgh, NY |access-date=March 1, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170514132910/http://www.airborneparkspeedway.com/ |archive-date=May 14, 2017 |url-status=live |df=mdy-all }}</ref> He also competed in the 2005 [[BriSCA Formula 1 Stock Cars|British Stock Car Association]] (BriSCA) Formula One Championship of the World, but did not finish.<ref>{{cite news|title=Phil Scott scores wire-to-wire victory|date=June 14, 2010|publisher=Barre-Montpelier (Vt.) Times-Argus|url=http://timesargus.com/article/20100614/SPORTS/6140323/1004/SPORTS|access-date=June 14, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100617184307/http://timesargus.com/article/20100614/SPORTS/6140323/1004/SPORTS|archive-date=June 17, 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On July 6, 2017, Scott won the Thunder Road Late Model Series feature race; he started from the pole, and the victory was his first since 2013.<ref>{{cite news |date=July 7, 2017 |title=Scott flying high at Thunder Road |url=http://www.timesargus.com/articles/scott-flying-high-at-thunder-road/ |work=Times Argus |location=Barre, VT |ref={{sfnRef|"Scott flying high at Thunder Road"}} |access-date=July 7, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170707201433/http://www.timesargus.com/articles/scott-flying-high-at-thunder-road/ |archive-date=July 7, 2017 |url-status=live |df=mdy-all }}</ref> He participated in a limited number of Thunder Road events in 2019, and won the June 27, 2019, LMS feature race.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thunderroadvt.com/home/2019/6/27/dragon-scott-add-to-thunder-road-legacies-on-ccv-night |title=Dragon, Scott Add to Thunder Road Legacies on CCV Night |last=Thunder Road Staff |date=June 27, 2019 |website=Thunder Road VT.com |publisher=Thunder Road Speedbowl |location=Barre, VT}}</ref> In July 2022, Scott competed in the Governor's Cup 150, in which he finished 23rd.<ref>{{cite news |date=July 14, 2022 |title=Corliss Cashes in for a Three-Peat in Vermont Lottery Governor's Cup 150 |url=https://www.thunderroadvt.com/home/2022/7/14/corliss-cashes-in-for-a-three-peat-in-vermont-lottery-governors-cup-150 |work=Thunder Road VT.com |location=Barre, VT}}</ref> As of July 2019, he has 31 career wins, the most in Late Model division history.<ref>{{cite news |date=June 28, 2019 |title=Vermont Governor Wins Late Model Race at Thunder Road |url=https://speed51.com/vermont-governor-wins-late-model-race-at-thunder-road/ |work=Speed51.com |location=Concord, NC}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Scott has been married three times, first to Jane Manosh, and later to Angela Wright.<ref>{{cite news |date=April 11, 1982 |title=Wedding Announcements: Manosh-Scott |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-times-argus-scott-manosh/127533134/ |work=[[Barre Montpelier Times Argus]] |location=Barre, VT |page=Section 3, page 4 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=February 6, 2000 |title=Wedding Announcements: Scott-Wright |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/rutland-daily-herald-marriage-of-wright/127533325/ |work=[[Rutland Herald]] |location=Rutland, VT |page=E6 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> He lives in [[Berlin, Vermont]], with his wife Diana McTeague Scott.<ref name="philscott.org" /> He has two adult daughters.<ref name="philscott.org">{{Cite web|url=http://www.philscott.org/meet-phil/|title=About Phil Scott – Early Years, Personal History & More|website=Phil Scott For Governor|language=en-US|access-date=April 29, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160421084647/http://www.philscott.org/meet-phil/|archive-date=April 21, 2016|url-status=live|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Vermont General Assembly |date=2001 |title=Vermont Legislative Directory and State Manual |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yiH0AAAAMAAJ&q=%22phil+scott%22+vermont+angie+wright |location=Montpelier, VT |publisher=Vermont Secretary of State |page=155 |access-date=September 5, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180905103623/https://books.google.de/books?id=yiH0AAAAMAAJ&q=%22phil+scott%22+vermont+angie+wright&dq=%22phil+scott%22+vermont+angie+wright&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjRqqbEkKPdAhWJCcAKHY7SCHIQ6AEIJzAA |archive-date=September 5, 2018 |url-status=live |df=mdy-all }}</ref>

